[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Workflow Control: pm.setNextRequest() in Postman"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Postman executes requests in the exact order they are arranged inside a collection or folder. However, real-world API testing scenarios often require conditional logic, looping, and skipping steps. The pm.setNextRequest() function allows you to dynamically control the execution flow of your Postman collection."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How pm.setNextRequest() Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The pm.setNextRequest() method accepts a string containing either the name of the target request or its unique ID. It must be placed inside the 'Tests' or 'Pre-request Script' section of a request. When Postman finishes executing the current request, it looks at this command to determine which request to run next, overriding the default sequential order."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Syntax"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"pm.setNextRequest('Get User Details');"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation Example"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where you first run an 'Authentication' request. If authentication fails, you want to skip the rest of the workflow. If it succeeds, you proceed to a 'Create Order' request."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"let jsonData = pm.response.json();

if (jsonData.isAuthenticated === true) {
    pm.setNextRequest('Create Order');
} else {
    // Stop the collection run entirely
    pm.setNextRequest(null);
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Rules and Limitations"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: pm.setNextRequest() only works when running a collection via the Postman Collection Runner or Newman. It has no effect when executing individual requests in the main Postman tab interface."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Queueing: If you call pm.setNextRequest() multiple times within the same request, the last set value takes precedence."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Termination: Passing null to pm.setNextRequest(null) will immediately stop the collection run after the current request finishes."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using pm.setNextRequest() transforms Postman from a simple API client into a powerful automated testing suite. By leveraging conditional statements and loops, you can simulate complex user journeys and edge cases efficiently."}]
